    "lawCode": "Add §1399-mm-1, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S428B\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to prohibiting the\nsale and distribution of flavored e-liquid for use in e-cigarettes\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends the Public Health Law by adding a new\nsection 1399-mm-1, which defines the terms \"flavored e-liquid\" and\n\"characterizing flavor\" for the purposes of the new section and bans the\nsale and distribution of any flavored e-liquid or e-cigarette in New\nYork State, other than tobacco flavored e-liquid or e-cigarettes.\nViolators of this prohibition would be subject to a civil fine of not\nmore than five hundred dollars.\n\nSection two of the bill establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe United States is in the midst of a rapidly worsening epidemic of\ne-cigarette use by minors. According to the 2019 National Youth Tobacco\nSurvey, more than 5 million youth report current e-cigarette use (having\nused e-cigarettes in the past 30 days), an increase of about 1.4 million\nsince 2018, and nearly 3 million since 2017. Nearly a million of those\nyouth report daily use of e-cigarettes.\n\nResearch published in the New England Journal of Medicine based on\nNational Institute of Health (NIH) data backs up those findings. That\nresearch showed that 1 in 4 high school seniors had vaped in the past 30\ndays (up from 1 in 10 in 2017) and 40.5% of high school seniors reported\nthey had tried vaping at least once (up from 25% in 2017).  The same\nresearch found that the percentage of eighth graders who had vaped in\nthe past 30 days has roughly tripled since 2017 (from 3.5% to 9%).\n\nThe New York Youth Tobacco Survey 2008-2018 tells us that here in New\nYork, 44.9% of high schoolers have tried vaping at least once and 27.4%\nof high schoolers are current users.\n\nThese findings are particularly troubling because mounting evidence\ndemonstrates that e-cigarettes and other vaping products are harmful to\nthe health of both children and adults alike. The U.S. was rocked by a\nwave of lung injuries and deaths linked to vaping beginning in the\nsummer of 2019. As of December 17, 2019, the CDC has confirmed 2,506\nhospitalized U.S. cases of E-cigarette or Vaping Product Use-Associated\nLung Injury (EVALI) and 54 deaths. Though research led by the CDC and\nNew York's own Wadsworth Labs has found that an additive called vitamin\nE acetate sometimes found in THC vaping products is \"closely associated\nwith\" these injuries, a CDC spokesperson also stressed that other chemi-\ncals could cause injury, saying \"a persistent small proportion of EVALI\ncases do not report use of THC-containing vaping products.\"\n\nSetting aside the EVALI cases, recent studies examining the health\nimpacts of e-cigarettes have linked their use to exposure to toxic chem-\nicals such as chromium, nickel, and lead found in the devices' heating\nsystems and other chemicals in found in e-cigarette aerosols linked to\nheart disease, respiratory disease, and cancer.\nStudies have also linked e-cigarette use to bronchiolitis (\"popcorn\nlung\") or scarring of the lungs; endothelial dysfunction (which often\nprecedes cardiovascular disease); wheezing and related respiratory symp-\ntoms; effects normally associated with the development of COPD; worsen-\ning of heart disease risk factors; and decreased blood flow to the\nheart.\n\nGiven how little time our public health experts have had to comprehen-\nsively study the effects of these devices over a sustained period of\ntime, these effects are likely the tip of the iceberg.\n\nAt a public hearing held by the Senate in November 2019 to examine this\nissue, officials from the New York State and New York City Departments\nof Health testified about the short and long-term health risks of\nvaping, and urged measures to restrict flavored e-cigarettes as a way to\nreduce the appeal of these products to minors.\n\nStudies consistently show that flavored e-cigarettes and other tobacco\nproducts are more appealing to children, and the use of flavored e-ci-\ngarettes makes both children and adults more likely to continue to vape,\nto vape more often and more heavily, and to initiate onto traditional\ncigarettes.\n\nIn fact, a 2018 survey of roughly 7,000 youth and young adult e-cigar-\nette and other tobacco users by researchers affiliated with the Univer-\nsity of Texas found that over 80% of respondents reported using flavored\nproducts, and three-fourths of those users said they would no longer use\nthe product if it was not flavored.\n\nE-cigarette companies have directly targeted ads for these appealing\nflavored products to children, both online and through physical adver-\ntising. In fact, the FDA has found that at least one major manufacturer\nof e-cigarettes had illegally marketed their products by implying that\nthey were safer than traditional cigarettes, even going so far as to\ncall their products \"totally safe\" during a presentation to students at\na school.\n\nThis predatory, deceptive marketing and these appealing flavored\nproducts have successfully misled many young people into thinking that\ne-cigarettes are safe. Many youth are alarmingly unaware that not only\ndo the vast majority of e-cigarettes contain nicotine, some e-cigarette\npods contain as much nicotine as an entire pack of cigarettes and can\ndeliver it in much higher doses.\n\nNew York State has made enormous progress in reducing conventional ciga-\nrette use over the past few decades. However, the explosion in e-cigar-\nette use by young people has the potential to addict a whole new gener-\nation to nicotine and cause numerous adverse health impacts to our young\npeople as they develop and grow.\n\nFor years, the FDA has consistently failed to take decisive action on\nthis issue, despite abundant warning signs that e-cigarette use by\nminors was spiraling out of control.\n\nThankfully, states have the authority to fill the gap in regulation.  As\nof January 2020, at least seven states (including New York) have enacted\nemergency rules to restrict or ban the sale of flavored e-cigarettes,\nand Massachusetts recently passed a law to prohibit the sale of all\nflavored tobacco products. The New York City Council also passed a law\nin November 2019 to ban the sale of flavored e-cigarettes. New York\nState should do the same to protect the health of children and adults\nalike.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019: 5.428-A (Hoylman) - Died on Third Reading\n2018: 5.8610 (Hannon) - Died on Third Reading\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
